      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find \\(x^2 + y^2\\), we start by using the given information about the arithmetic and geometric means of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).\n\nFirst, we know that the arithmetic mean of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) is 18. This can be written as:\n\\[\n\\frac{x + y}{2} = 18\n\\]\nMultiplying both sides by 2, we get:\n\\[\nx + y = 36\n\\]\n\nNext, we know that the geometric mean of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) is \\(\\sqrt{92}\\). This can be written as:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{xy} = \\sqrt{92}\n\\]\nSquaring both sides, we get:\n\\[\nxy = 92\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(x^2 + y^2\\). We use the identity:\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 = (x + y)^2 - 2xy\n\\]\nSubstituting the values we know, we get:\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 = 36^2 - 2 \\cdot 92\n\\]\nCalculating \\(36^2\\), we get:\n\\[\n36^2 = 1296\n\\]\nCalculating \\(2 \\cdot 92\\), we get:\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 92 = 184\n\\]\nSo,\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 = 1296 - 184 = 1112\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(x^2 + y^2\\) is \\(\\boxed{1112}\\).",
